<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bengal Presidency</h1><p class="st-meta">1765 CE – 1947 CE · capital Calcutta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's largest province in India, governed from Fort William at Calcutta, which was the capital of British India until 1911.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-william-kolkata">Fort William</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> around 1700 and made it the seat of a presidency. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Plassey</a> in 1757 and the grant of the diwani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in 1765, the Bengal Presidency became a territorial government; from 1773 its governor was also Governor-General over the other presidencies.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The presidency once stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and governed vast populations through district collectors. The Permanent Settlement of 1793 fixed land revenue with zamindars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> became the commercial and intellectual capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British India</a>, the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Ram Mohan Roy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>. The famine of 1770 and that of 1943 killed millions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1905; protests forced reunion in 1911, when the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1947 Bengal was partitioned again, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bangladesh">East Bengal</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a>, later becoming Bangladesh.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bengal-presidency.jpg" alt="Bengal Presidency" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Bengal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1765 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calcutta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Warren Hastings, Lord Curzon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Presidency of Fort William, Bengal Province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=bengal-presidency" aria-label="Bengal Presidency on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7044 1346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bengal Presidency lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7364 1602L7364 1601M7364 1602L7364 1602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7364 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7364 1602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=bengal-presidency">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

